{"author":{"id":"8d7a7fea5b07a0d5d081edd516d55afeb51ce9c18b2b2be6ceb30eb17cd9b547"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794386612,"nonce":"5qPItUUHjUDZEZkMtZN6wSkdVO0=","message":"Revised fix, branch fix-input-signals 887cb070 (not yet approved). The graph is no longer changed to hand out removed nodes. Dropping data releases its signals with try_lock on the graph: at once when the lock is free, otherwise (the drop was caused by the graph replacing or removing a value: set, update, a recomputed computed, a disposed effect) the ids are queued and disposed by dispose_pending(), run where a scope is disposed and where a build starts. This covers the further paths found in review (set, update, State::set, recompute). Running the queue after every write or inside Computed::try_get was tried and hung the layout tests (it can run while the binding registry is locked; the dispose notifier takes that lock), so those hooks are not used. Leaks: callbacks stored in a text input's own data held the data (OTP slots, number input step and change callbacks, time picker step and submit callbacks); they hold it weakly now. blinc_cn/tests/component_signal_leaks.rs builds, opens and disposes 13 components 50 times and requires the live signal, computed and effect counts not to grow between the 10th and 50th cycle (input_otp, number_input, time_picker failed before the change; select needs the per-frame instance counters reset, as the runner does). blinc_core/tests/data_signals.rs covers the hang paths and the live signal count after 1000 rebuilds. Tests: blinc_core, blinc_layout lib, blinc_cn all green, blinc_app lib green (also with wayland-frame-gate), cargo check x86_64-pc-windows-gnu for core, layout and cn.","files":null}]}